{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Apply Print Settings}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Apply_Print_Settings}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Apply Print Settings}
\fs36 Apply Print Settings
\par \pard \fs24 
\par This option is for use with Network Printers and is loca{\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Images Across the Page}
ted on the \uldb Network Printers Tab\plain\fs24 {\v Network_Printer_Tab}, and is activated when the \uldb Enable Network Printer\plain\fs24 {\v Enable_transfers_to_Network_Printer} option is checked.
\par 
\par When this button is pressed, the Image Archive program will attempt to log in to the network printer ready to transfer any selected Image files. The print formatting options are actually applied just before the images are sent to the printer.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Archive Attribute}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 1MP7OUG}
\pard\keepn \fs36 Archive Attribute
\par \pard \fs24 
\par Each filename under the DOS operating system has a number of attributes associated with it, these can be any of :
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab Normal (default)
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab Read-only
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab Hidden
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab System file
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab Archive - File has changed since last backup
\par 
\par \pard\tx355 ImgArc monitors the Archive attributes on image files, and transfers those files which have the archive attribute set. Once a file has been successfully transferred, the archive attribute on this file is cleared.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 ImgArc will by default not transfer image files with this bit set. The user may however \uldb change the archive attribute\plain\fs24 {\v Changing_the_Archive_Attribute} on any image file manually, or override this functionality by using the \uldb Include Files Already Transferred\plain\fs24 {\v Include_Files_Already_Transferred} check-box on the \uldb Image File Source Tab\plain\fs24 {\v Image_File_Source_Tab}.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Archived Images}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} XYKH1}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Archive Bit}
\fs36 Archived Images
\par \pard \fs24 
\par When logged in, this list box will contain a directory listing of the \uldb host computer\plain\fs24 {\v 3H.QV4}, using the specified \uldb directory path\plain\fs24 {\v 1BZPJX} that is assigned to the remote \uldb user account\plain\fs24 {\v 3HIUH2}.
\par 
\par Unlike the local \uldb SEM Image direcory\plain\fs24 {\v SEM_Image_Files}, all files in the remote directory are listed. The format of each entry is described below :
\par 
\par \f2 -rw-rw-r--   1 37417    996        39754 Jun  9 16:33 image1.jpg\f1 
\par 
\par The first four fields describe the file access attributes on the remote system, these are followed in order by :
\par \pard 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab File size in bytes : This may differ between machines, and is simply a replaction of the amount of storage space required on that system to hold the file
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab Month, Day and time that the file was created
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab The File name itself
\par 
\par \pard\tx355 The next two example entries represent the current directory and the directory above the current directory (the parent directory) respectively.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \f2 drwxrws---   2 37417    996          512 Jun  9 16:33 .
\par \pard\tx355 drwxr-xr-x 940 0        996        15360 Jun 11 08:57 ..
\par \pard\tx355 \f1 
\par \pard\tx355 Double click on the '..' filename to move to the parent directory. Note that the \uldb current Image Directory\plain\fs24 {\v 1BZPJX} and the Archived Images list refresh to reflect this change.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Browse}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} XQHW0R}
\pard\keepn \fs36 Browse
\par \pard \fs24 
\par This option allows the user to specifiy another log file name that will hold the contents of \uldb the user log file\plain\fs24 {\v User_Log_File}. A file selection dialog box similar to the one show below will appear to make file selection easier :
\par 
\par \{bmc bm1.bmp\}
\par 
\par The log file is always stored in the SEM User directory which is displayed on the ImgArc \uldb Source Tab\plain\fs24 {\v Image_File_Source_Tab}.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Cancel All File Transfers}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 9A3XVB}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Archived Images}
\fs36 Cancel All File Transfers
\par \pard \fs24 
\par When a \uldb file transfer\plain\fs24 {\v 5VFV.V} is in progress, clicking this button will cancel the current file being transferred, along with all other subsequent file transfers for that session. A prompt will appear asking the user to confirm this action. If the cancel operation is confirmed, the partially copied file on the \uldb remote host\plain\fs24 {\v 3H.QV4} computer will be automatically deleted. 
\par 
\par Click the \uldb Cancel button\plain\fs24 {\v 3M0LA4C} to cancel the current file being transferred only.
\par 
\par \i\ul Note:\plain\i\fs24  When transferring Images to the network printer (When the \uldb Enable Network Printer\plain\i\fs24 {\v Enable_transfers_to_Network_Printer} option is checked, which is located on the \uldb Network Printers Tab\plain\i\fs24 {\v Network_Printer_Tab}.), the network printer will not include any of the remaining images in its printout(s). To Cancel the print job entirely, use the \uldb Cancel Print Job\plain\i\fs24 {\v Cancel_Current_Job} button.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Cancel Current Job}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Cancel_Current_Job}
\pard\keepn \fs36 Cancel Current Job
\par \pard \fs24 
\par This option is for use with Network Printers and is located on the \uldb Network Printers Tab\plain\fs24 {\v Network_Printer_Tab}, and is activated when the \uldb Enable Network Printer\plain\fs24 {\v Enable_transfers_to_Network_Printer} option is checked.
\par 
\par When logged in to the network printer, pressing this button will transfer a command to the Network printer to abort any print jobs in progress. Any Images transferred to the printer prior to this command will not be printed.
\par 
\par \i\ul Note :\plain\i\fs24  Use this command with caution!\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Cancel Image Transfer}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 3M0LA4C}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Browse}
\fs36 Cancel Image Transfer
\par \pard \fs24 
\par When a \uldb file transfer\plain\fs24 {\v 5VFV.V} is in progress, clicking this button will cancel the current file being transferred. A prompt will appear asking the user to confirm this action. If the cancel operation is confirmed, the partially copied file on the \uldb remote host\plain\fs24 {\v 3H.QV4} computer will be automatically deleted. There may be a brief pause whilst this operation is completed.
\par 
\par File transfer will resume with the next image file in the list. To cancel all transfers, click the \uldb Cancel All\plain\fs24 {\v 9A3XVB} button. 
\par \pard 
\par \i\ul Note :\plain\i\fs24  The default behaviour of an FTP operation is to over-write files of the same name. If a file transfer operation has already started to copy a file over the top of an existing file, the existing file will also be unavailable when the cancel operation is used. The \uldb user\plain\i\fs24 {\v 3HIUH2} can use the \uldb Destination Tab\plain\i\fs24 {\v Image_File_Destination_Tab} to check the files in their \uldb target directory\plain\i\fs24 {\v XYKH1} during the \uldb setup mode of operation\plain\i\fs24 {\v Modes_of_Operation} of ImgArc.
\par 
\par When transferring Images to the network printer (When the \uldb Enable Network Printer\plain\i\fs24 {\v Enable_transfers_to_Network_Printer} option is checked, which is located on the \uldb Network Printers Tab\plain\i\fs24 {\v Network_Printer_Tab}.), the network printer will not include this image in its printout(s). To Cancel the print job entirely, use the \uldb Cancel Print Job\plain\i\fs24 {\v Cancel_Current_Job} button.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Change Dir}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 1LJZ_DP}
\pard\keepn \fs36 Change Dir
\par \pard \fs24 
\par This button causes ImgArc to change the Destination Image Directory to that specified by the \uldb FTP Image Directory\plain\fs24 {\v 1BZPJX} input box. If a directory change is successful, then the \uldb Image Archive List\plain\fs24 {\v XYKH1} will be updated. This setting then becomes the default directory for \uldb the user\plain\fs24 {\v 3HIUH2} to log in to on the \uldb remote host\plain\fs24 {\v 3H.QV4}.
\par 
\par If \uldb the user\plain\fs24 {\v 3HIUH2} does not have sufficient privilege, or the directory specified is invalid, then an \uldb FTP error\plain\fs24 {\v Errors_Help} is reported both in the \uldb Image Archive list\plain\fs24 {\v XYKH1} and in the \uldb Status Bar\plain\fs24 {\v The_Status_Bar}.
\par \pard 
\par \i\ul Hint :\plain\i\fs24  To set a directory automatically, those entries in the \uldb Image Archive List\plain\i\fs24 {\v XYKH1} that represent directories can be double clicked on.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
