\par \pard \fs24 
\par The LEO-32 UIF Currently Supports a number of output file formats. These Include :
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab TIFF - Tagged Image File Format Version 5.0
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab JPG - Image Files saved using the JPEG Compression algorithm
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab BMP - Windows Bitmap File Format
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \i\ul Note :\plain\i\fs24  The SEM Image File List on the ImgMgr \uldb Source Tab\plain\i\fs24 {\v Image_File_Source_Tab} is not a complete list of images in the Image Directory, but displays only those images with their \uldb archive attribute\plain\i\fs24 {\v 1MP7OUG} set.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Scaling Selection}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Scaling_Selection}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Image Dir}
\fs36 Scaling Selection
\par \pard \fs24 
\par This option is for use with Network Printers and is located on the \uldb Network Printers Tab\plain\fs24 {\v Network_Printer_Tab}, and is activated when the \uldb Enable Network Printer\plain\fs24 {\v Enable_transfers_to_Network_Printer} option is checked.
\par 
\par This option sets the method by which images are scaled down and pixels removed to allow an Image to be fitted into its position on the printed page. The current methods available for scaling Images are any of :
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab \b BOX\plain\fs24  : Box scaling is the fastest of the scaling algorithms. It is implemented using pixel replication. Box should be used for images containing text with block letters in which text must remain sharp. Artifacts : Box tends to produce blocky-looking prints, especially when large scale factors are used.
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab \b BILINEAR\plain\fs24  : Bilinear scaling is the second-fastest scaling routine and is the \b default\plain\fs24  printer setting. It employs a linear interpolation to determine the output image. Bilinear scaling provides ver good scaling for most applications where scale factors are relatively small (4X or less). A great amount of optimization effort was put into the bilinear algorithm. Artifacts : Block letters will have fuzzy edges. Bilinear results in a slight decrease in dynamic range for large scale factors.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab \b BICUBIC\plain\fs24  : The Bicubic algorithm produces the smoothest output, but tends to smooth over fine details. The filter function is a cubic B-spline filter. This routine requires the same processing time as Mitchell. Bicubic is recommended for applications where the smoothest output is required. Artifacts : Images may appear somewhat blurry due to the smoothing of the filter. Block letters will have fuzzy edges.
\par \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab \b MITCHELL\plain\fs24  : Mitchell uses a cubic Mitchell function to produce a very smooth output while maintaining dynamic range and sharpness. Mitchell processing takes approximately twice the processing time as Bilinear. Mitchell can be used for any scaling application, especially when scaling factors are 2X or greater. Artifacts : Block letters will have fuzzy edges.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\tab \b LANCZOS\plain\fs24  : Lanczos uses a filter based on the sinc function. This is the most theoretically correct filter and produces the best output for photographic images that do not have sharp transitions in them. However, Lanczos will produce ripple artefacts especially for block text, due to aliasing. Lanczos also requires three times the processing time of Bilinear. Lanczos is not recommended except in very rare applications using band-limited photographic images with no sharp edges. Artifacts : Lanczos produces ripple artifacts around sharp edges, such as found with text. This algorithm is not recommended for most applications.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 This option is actioned when the \uldb Apply Print Settings\plain\fs24 {\v Apply_Print_Settings} Button is Pressed.
\par \page
